---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import TimingComparison from "../../components/TimingComparison.astro";
import StorageComparison from "../../components/StorageComparison.astro";
import { browserTimings } from "../../components/comparison-data";
---
<Base
  title="Two Rust photo apps in the browser — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="LightCraft is another wonderful Lightroom replacement written in Rust that runs in a browser tab. Here is what I learned when I put it next to LightPhotos. I include some measurements, but they are not a fully equal comparison."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-10-07">October 7, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Two Rust photo apps in the browser</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          <a href="https://github.com/storytold/lightcraft">LightCraft</a>
          is another wonderful Lightroom replacement, written in Rust, that
          runs in a browser tab. LightPhotos does the same but with some major differences.
          Here are some comparisons I made.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 8 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          Both apps are Rust and egui compiled to WebAssembly. The compressed
          downloads are 3.6&nbsp;MB and 3.8&nbsp;MB. Performance-wise, they are very similar,
          where LightPhotos is slightly faster. The major difference is how they handle your photos:
        </p>
        <ul class="lp-article-list">
          <li>LightPhotos reads the photos from your disk in place and saves its data in a <code>.lightphotos</code> folder inside it. This only works in Chrome and Edge.</li>
          <li>LightCraft copies the photos into the browser's own storage. This works in every modern browser, but it means a second copy of every photo in your browser's storage.</li>
        </ul>
      </aside>

      <TimingComparison
        id="start-demo"
        title="When the photo is sharp and when the screen settles"
        scale={4}
        milestones={["First image", "Sharp photo", "Whole screen settled"]}
        scenarios={browserTimings}
        note="Fixed 0–4 s scale for both actions. Medians of five runs in Chrome, full screen on a 4K display. Screenshots were taken about every 70 ms; 0.07 s is the first frame after the key press."
      />

      <h2>Same language, different answers</h2>
      <p>
        Both apps are Rust from top to bottom. Both draw their interface
        with egui on a canvas, and each web version is its desktop app
        compiled to WebAssembly. So the language does not decide much. The
        apps still differ on storage, threads, graphics and toolchain, and
        LightPhotos used LightCraft's layout until late September (see <a
        href="/blogs/challenges-with-webassembly.html">challenges with
        WebAssembly</a>).
      </p>
      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col" class="lp-compare-pick">LightPhotos</th>
              <th scope="col">LightCraft</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Your photos</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Read in place from a folder you pick</td>
              <td>Copied into the browser's own storage on import</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Parallel work</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">6 decode threads that share one memory, with the same queues as the desktop app</td>
              <td>Up to 4 Web Workers, each with its own memory. Jobs go in as JSON, pixels come back</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Graphics</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">WebGPU, the newer API</td>
              <td>WebGL2, which more browsers support</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Browsers</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Chrome, Edge</td>
              <td>No WebGPU or folder access needed</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Rust toolchain</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Nightly, standard library rebuilt</td>
              <td>Stable</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Server headers</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Required (cross-origin isolation, COOP/COEP)</td>
              <td>Optional</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">What a RAW shows at fit</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">The RAW data, developed at the window's size, with your edits. The camera's JPEG while that develop runs</td>
              <td>The RAW data, developed, with your edits</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">The photos on either side</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Developed ahead of time</td>
              <td>Prepared ahead of time</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">RAW decoder</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">rawler (LGPL-2.1), disclosed with its license</td>
              <td>Its own</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">License</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">MIT or Apache-2.0, plus rawler under LGPL-2.1</td>
              <td>MIT or Apache-2.0</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">After a reload</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Reopen Session continues from where you stopped</td>
              <td>Library is still there</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Open in several tabs</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Allowed, with no guard against two tabs that overwrite each other's saves</td>
              <td>One tab at a time</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Import is the biggest difference</h2>
      <p>
        The row in the table that you feel first is the one about your
        photos. The two apps answer "where are my photos?" in opposite ways.
      </p>

      <StorageComparison id="import-demo" />
      <p>
        LightPhotos does not import. <strong>Open Folder</strong> calls the
        browser's <code>showDirectoryPicker</code>, you grant access to one
        folder, and the app reads the files where they are. Nothing is
        copied. Ratings, edits and thumbnails go into a hidden
        <code>.lightphotos</code> folder inside the folder you picked (see <a
        href="/blogs/where-your-data-lives.html">where your data lives</a>),
        so you can see them in Finder or Explorer and they move with the
        folder.
      </p>
      <p>
        The cost is that this API exists only in Chrome and Edge,
        and after a reload the browser asks you to grant access again,
        unless you chose to allow it on every visit.
      </p>
      <p>
        LightCraft imports. You pick files with the standard file dialog, or
        drop them on the window. It reads the bytes of each file and writes
        them into the browser's own file system, the Origin Private File
        System (OPFS), under a hash of their content. The catalog, presets
        and settings go there too, or into IndexedDB where OPFS cannot be
        written. This works in every modern browser, with no permission
        prompt. A reload opens the same library, and a photo imported twice
        is stored once.
      </p>
      <p>
        The cost is a second copy of every photo. The twenty Sony RAW files in this test are
        498&nbsp;MB, so importing this set into an empty library stores a
        second 498&nbsp;MB in browser storage. That storage has a quota, it is invisible in Finder or
        Explorer, and the browser can evict it under storage pressure, so
        LightCraft asks for persistent storage and warns you when it does
        not get it. If you clear the site's data, the library goes with it.
        Your original files are safe, but to get edited photos out, you
        export them.
      </p>
      <p>
        LightPhotos reads the folder you choose and saves ratings and edits
        alongside the originals. It avoids duplicating the photos, but
        requires Chrome or Edge. LightCraft keeps a separate library in
        browser storage, which supports file import across more browsers
        and retains the library after a reload. Clearing site data removes
        that library and edits you have not exported; the originals on disk
        remain.
      </p>

      <h2>How I measured</h2>
      <p>
        I built each app from its current code and served it from a local
        address. Playwright drove Chrome 154 on an M4 Pro Mac with
        48&nbsp;GB of memory. The window filled a 4K display at its native
        size, so the page was 3840 by 2072 pixels. The photos were twenty
        RAW files from a Sony A7 II, 24 megapixels and about 25&nbsp;MB
        each. Each run used a new browser profile, so neither app had seen
        the photos before.
      </p>
      <p>
        The script takes a screenshot about every 70&nbsp;ms and keeps each
        frame that changes. For each key press, I record two times. The
        first is when the photo area stops changing. The second is when the
        whole screen stops changing, side panels included. Each number is
        the median of five runs. For an open from the grid, the script waits
        until the screen is still for 4&nbsp;s, because the sharp photo can
        come late. The method is coarse: 0.07&nbsp;s is the first frame
        after the key. So compare the apps with each other, not with a
        stopwatch.
      </p>
      <p>
        An earlier version of this post timed only the whole screen, in a
        1600 by 1000 window. It gave 0.97&nbsp;s for the next photo in
        LightCraft. The frames show that the photo was sharp in the first
        frame. The time came from the histogram.
      </p>

      <h2>The numbers</h2>
      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col" class="lp-compare-pick">LightPhotos</th>
              <th scope="col">LightCraft</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Download, compressed (brotli)</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">3.6&nbsp;MB</td>
              <td>3.8&nbsp;MB</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">WebAssembly file, uncompressed</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">14.4&nbsp;MB</td>
              <td>17.1&nbsp;MB</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Fill the grid with 20 RAW thumbnails, total</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">1.4&nbsp;s</td>
              <td>2.3&nbsp;s</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Next photo: sharp photo on screen (median of 40)</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">0.07&nbsp;s</td>
              <td>0.12&nbsp;s</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Next photo: the whole screen stops changing</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">0.13&nbsp;s</td>
              <td>1.70&nbsp;s</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Open from the grid: sharp photo on screen</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">1.37&nbsp;s</td>
              <td>3.6&nbsp;s</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Memory afterwards, the page's process</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">2.0&nbsp;GB</td>
              <td>2.8&nbsp;GB</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        LightCraft includes a full editing pipeline and its own RAW decoder,
        but its download is only 0.2&nbsp;MB larger. Its release builds also
        include a Japanese font, which I removed for this test. The memory
        number is for the page's own process. That process holds the main
        thread, the workers and the LightPhotos decode threads. When the
        LightCraft grid stops changing, it shows only a few of the RAW
        thumbnails, because it scrolls to follow the import. LightPhotos
        shows all 20.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the numbers differ</h2>
      <p>
        The camera's embedded JPEG is 1616&nbsp;px wide. Full screen, the
        photo is about 2700&nbsp;px wide in both apps, so the JPEG is too
        small for a sharp photo. Thus both apps develop the sensor data at
        fit. The colours at fit then come from the same place as the colours
        at 100%.
      </p>
      <p>
        Both apps prepare the photos on either side while you look at the
        current one. LightPhotos develops them on its decode threads.
        LightCraft decodes them in its Web Workers. So for the next photo,
        the work is done before you press the key, and both apps show the
        sharp photo in about 0.1&nbsp;s. LightCraft shows a preview for
        about 0.07&nbsp;s first. Then its histogram changes until about
        1.7&nbsp;s after the key. The photo does not change in that time.
      </p>
      <p>
        A photo that you open from the grid is not prepared. LightPhotos
        shows the camera's JPEG at 0.15&nbsp;s. Then it develops the sensor
        data and shows the sharp photo at 1.37&nbsp;s. LightCraft shows a
        preview at 0.06&nbsp;s and the sharp photo at 3.6&nbsp;s. In two of
        five runs, LightCraft was faster, at 1.25&nbsp;s. I did not find the
        cause.
      </p>
      <p>
        The grid is closer. The LightPhotos decode threads take thumbnails
        from one shared queue, and the photo on screen gets priority.
        LightCraft copies all 498&nbsp;MB into browser storage while it
        imports. That copy is part of its 2.3&nbsp;s. It also keeps the
        library after a reload.
      </p>

      <h2>Why LightCraft uses more memory</h2>
      <p>
        Each LightCraft worker is a separate copy of the program with its
        own memory, and WebAssembly memory never shrinks after it grows. The
        main thread also keeps recently used originals, the 498&nbsp;MB of
        RAW files. Four workers plus that come to 2.8&nbsp;GB. With a photo
        zoomed to 1:1, it reached 3.4&nbsp;GB.
      </p>
      <p>
        Shared memory has a cost too. Each LightPhotos decode thread needs
        about 300&nbsp;MB of scratch space for a RAW file, all from one heap
        with a limit of 4&nbsp;GB. With ten threads, memory peaked at
        3.4&nbsp;GB. So I limited the browser build to six threads, which
        keeps the page at 2.0&nbsp;GB.
      </p>

      <h2>Wrapping up</h2>
      <p>
        LightPhotos relies on your own folders and shared memory, so it feels
        quick when you go through a shoot. LightCraft takes on the whole RAW
        pipeline and a full Lightroom-style feature set, keeps its library
        in the browser and needs no special server configuration.

        I look forward to seeing where it goes. Both also run on Mac, Windows and
        Linux, and that comparison is next.
      </p>

      <nav class="lp-article-nav" aria-label="More posts">
        <a class="lp-article-nav-prev" href="/blogs/exporting-to-your-own-immich-server.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Back</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">Exporting to your own Immich server</span>
        </a>
        <a class="lp-article-nav-next" href="/blogs/two-rust-photo-apps-on-the-mac.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Next</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">Two Rust photo apps on the Mac</span>
        </a>
      </nav>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>
